## Answer

This change modifies **one file**: `pkg/cmd/pr/status/status.go`, specifically the **`statusRun`** function.

**What it changes:** Currently, `statusRun` only populates `currentBranch`, `currentPRNumber`, and `currentPRHeadRef` when `opts.HasRepoOverride` is `false` (lines 85–96) — i.e., it skips looking up the current branch/PR entirely when the user passes `-R/--repo` to target a different repository. However, the output section that prints these values is unconditional (lines 134–150):

```go
shared.PrintHeader(opts.IO, "Current branch")
currentPR := prPayload.CurrentPR
if currentPR != nil && currentPR.State != "OPEN" && prPayload.DefaultBranch == currentBranch {
    currentPR = nil
}
if currentPR != nil {
    printPrs(opts.IO, 1, *currentPR)
} else if currentPRHeadRef == "" {
    shared.PrintMessage(opts.IO, "  There is no current branch")
} else {
    shared.PrintMessage(opts.IO, fmt.Sprintf("  There is no pull request associated with %s", cs.Cyan("["+currentPRHeadRef+"]")))
}
fmt.Fprintln(out)
```

Because those variables are never populated when `--repo` is used, this section misleadingly always prints "Current branch / There is no current branch" — even though the concept of a "current branch" is meaningless/irrelevant when querying a different repo via `--repo`.

The fix wraps this entire "Current branch" block in a check for `!opts.HasRepoOverride`, so the section is only rendered when the command is run against the local repo's default context (no `--repo` override), hiding it entirely when `--repo` is passed — matching the PR title "Hide 'current branch' for pr status --repo".

The corresponding test file `pkg/cmd/pr/status/status_test.go` would also need a new test case (e.g. exercising `runCommand` with a `--repo`/`-R` flag or `HasRepoOverride: true`) asserting that the "Current branch" header/body is absent from the output, but the functional change is confined to `statusRun` in `pkg/cmd/pr/status/status.go`.

