The change (feat #4248: line-range support for `gh browse`) modifies **`pkg/cmd/browse/browse.go`**, specifically the `parseFileArg` function:

```go
func parseFileArg(fileArg string) (string, error) {
	arr := strings.Split(fileArg, ":")
	if len(arr) > 2 {
		return "", fmt.Errorf("invalid use of colon\nUse 'gh browse --help' for more information about browse\n")
	}
	if len(arr) > 1 {
		if !isNumber(arr[1]) {
			return "", fmt.Errorf("invalid line number after colon\nUse 'gh browse --help' for more information about browse\n")
		}
		return arr[0] + "#L" + arr[1], nil
	}
	return arr[0], nil
}
```

Currently `parseFileArg` splits the selector on `:` and only accepts a single numeric line (e.g. `main.go:312` → `main.go#L312`), producing a GitHub anchor of `#L<n>`. To support `test.go:10-20`, this function needs to:

1. Accept a `<start>-<end>` form after the colon instead of (or in addition to) a plain integer — i.e. parse `arr[1]` for a hyphen-separated range.
2. Validate both the start and end are numbers (the existing `isNumber` check would need to change to handle a range string, likely by splitting on `-` and validating each part, or via a regex).
3. Build the GitHub line-range anchor format, which is `#L<start>-L<end>` (GitHub's URL fragment convention for ranges), instead of the current single-line `#L<n>`.

The helper `isNumber` (same file) would also need to change or be supplemented, since it currently just does `strconv.Atoi` on the whole string after the colon — that call would fail on `"10-20"` and incorrectly report "invalid line number after colon."

Correspondingly, **`pkg/cmd/browse/browse_test.go`** would need new/updated test cases in `Test_parseFileArg` (e.g. `"main.go:10-20"` → `"main.go#L10-L20"`) and possibly in `Test_runBrowse` (an end-to-end case with a range selector like `path/to/file.txt:10-20`) to cover the new behavior, plus updates to the command's help/examples text (the `Example` heredoc and `Annotations["help:arguments"]` in `NewCmdBrowse`) if the author documents the new syntax there.

No other files in the repository reference `parseFileArg` or `isNumber`, so the change is confined to this single command package.
